Developers and contact:
This benchmark was developed by Nikhil Jain as part of PPL's effort
to develop miniapplications that demonstrate utility of Charm++. It
mimics the short-ranged force computation (due to Lennard Jones potential)
in NAMD. It is roughly based on mol3d, developed by Edgar Solomonik and
Abhinav Bhatele, which is a benchmark based on NAMD. Phil Miller's guidance
was crucial in writing the SDAG routines. Later on Esteban Meneses and Xiang Ni
added fault tolerance to it. It has been used in many publications related
to Charm++. Contact nikhil.jain@acm.org for any bug report or comments.

GPU PORT
--------
This copy is a GPUDirect port of that benchmark, for work on communication-aware
GPU load balancing. Particle and force arrays are device resident for the whole
run: they are allocated once on the GPU, read and written only by kernels, and
exchanged between chares by device-to-device zerocopy. The only things that cross
to the host are per-bucket particle counts at migration steps and two energy
scalars at the first and last step.

What changed from the original, and why:

 * The CkMulticast section is gone, in both directions. A Cell used one section
   both to multicast positions to its computes and to collect their forces with
   CkReduction::sum_double. Charm++ reductions combine host buffers, so keeping
   that would have meant a D2H on every compute and an H2D on every cell, every
   step. Positions now go out as point-to-point nocopydevice sends and forces come
   back the same way, folded into the accumulator by a kernel. Each message
   carries the ordinal the cell handed out, so a returning force array lands in
   its own slot -- which is what the CkSectionInfo cookie used to do.

   Two consequences. The spanning tree is lost, so a cell now issues 27 unicasts
   instead of one tree'd multicast; there is no device-buffer multicast in the
   runtime, and the point-to-point edges are what the balancer's communication
   graph needs to see anyway. And forces sum in arrival order rather than tree
   order, so totals are no longer bit-identical run to run.

 * Particle migration runs on the device. The host-side sort in migrateParticles
   would have pulled the entire particle array back every MIGRATE_STEPCOUNT steps.
   binParticlesKernel classifies, wraps and compacts in one pass; the stayers are
   adopted by a pointer swap and the outgoing buckets are shipped as device
   buffers.

 * Granularity is coarser: KAWAY 1,1,1 and 800-1000 particles per cell, against
   2,2,1 and 100-250. See the occupancy table in defs.h -- at stock values a
   compute's pair kernel launches 40 blocks and cannot fill a GPU.

 * The pair kernel uses one block per atom with a shared-memory reduction, and
   does not exploit Newton's third law. Both choices are explained at the top of
   leanmd.cu.

 * The initial-velocity RNG was switched from srand48/drand48 to erand48. The
   originals share one global state, and every PE of an SMP build is a thread of
   one process, so Cell constructors on different PEs corrupted each other's
   sequence: initial energy differed on every run at +p4 and varied with PE count.
   The seeding reproduces what a single-PE stock run produced.

Validated against the unmodified CPU benchmark at matching granularity: initial
and final energies agree to all ten printed digits at +p1, +p2 and +p4, including
across migration steps.

TO COMPILE-
Do a make in this folder

TO RUN-
Either provide no arguments or provide all
./leanmd +p<Procs> <dimX,dimY,dimZ,steps,firstLBstep,LBPeriod>
Example - ./leanmd +p4 3 3 3 1001 20 20
Example - ./leanmd +p4 3 3 3 1001 20 20 +balancer GreedyRefineLB

READ ORDER
leanmd.ci - contains the most important aspects of the program, look for run()
function for each chare which is called right after constructor call for each
chare. It contains most of the parallel flow and control flow information.

leanmd.cu - the force, integration, energy and migration kernels.

leanmd_cuda.h - host-visible interface to those kernels, and the place where the
device-residency contract is written down.

Cell.* - contains the implementation of important functions attached with
Cell; focus on sendPositions(), accumulateForce() and binParticles()

Compute.* - contains the implementation for Compute; launchForces() does the
force computation and periodicShift() handles the wraparound.

Main.* - contains the constructor for Main chare; also the starting point
of the application; main chare passes control to run() of Main chare and
constructors of Cell.

physics.h - the original CPU force computation. No longer compiled; kept as the
reference the kernels in leanmd.cu were ported from.

defs.h - #define for constant values and some class declaration
